{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/19/2/246/pdf?version=1547114428"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"sels|camera_calibration_using_gray_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Seppe_Sels:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bart_Ribbens:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steve_Vanlanduit:","~Rudi_Penne1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s19020246"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/SelsRVP19,\n  author={Seppe Sels and Bart Ribbens and Steve Vanlanduit and Rudi Penne},\n  title={Camera Calibration Using Gray Code},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={19},\n  number={2},\n  pages={246},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s19020246}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In order to determine camera parameters, a calibration procedure involving the camera recordings of a checkerboard is usually performed. In this paper, we propose an alternative approach that uses Gray-code patterns displayed on an LCD screen. Gray-code patterns allow us to decode 3D location information of points of the LCD screen at every pixel in the camera image. This is in contrast to checkerboard patterns where the number of corresponding locations is limited to the number of checkerboard corners. We show that, for the case of a UEye CMOS camera, the precision of focal-length estimation is 1.5 times more precise than when using a standard calibration with a checkerboard pattern."},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration Using Gray Code"},"authors":{"value":["Seppe Sels","Bart Ribbens","Steve Vanlanduit","Rudi Penne"]}},"tmdate":1731503531918,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731503520838,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rudi_Penne1"],"forum":"3rCHCg0oJi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":227354,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731503531918,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3rCHCg0oJi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICSE 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|on_calibration_of_pretrained_code_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhenhao_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chaofeng_Sha:","~Xin_Peng6"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3639126"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icse/ZhouS024,\n  author={Zhenhao Zhou and Chaofeng Sha and Xin Peng},\n  title={On Calibration of Pre-trained Code Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={77:1-77:13},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3639126},\n  booktitle={ICSE},\n  crossref={conf/icse/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Pre-trained code models have achieved notable success in the field of Software Engineering (SE). However, existing studies have predominantly focused on improving model performance, with limited attention given to other critical aspects such as model calibration. Model calibration, which refers to the accurate estimation of predictive uncertainty, is a vital consideration in practical applications. Therefore, in order to advance the understanding of model calibration in SE, we conduct a comprehensive investigation into the calibration of pre-trained code models in this paper. Our investigation focuses on five pre-trained code models and four code understanding tasks, including analyses of calibration in both in-distribution and out-of-distribution settings. Several key insights are uncovered: (1) pre-trained code models may suffer from the issue of over-confidence; (2) temperature scaling and label smoothing are effective in calibrating code models in in-distribution data; (3) the issue of over-confidence in pre-trained code models worsens in different out-of-distribution settings, and the effectiveness of temperature scaling and label smoothing diminishes. All materials used in our experiments are available at https://github.com/queserasera22/Calibration-of-Pretrained-Code-Models."},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Pre-trained Code Models"},"authors":{"value":["Zhenhao Zhou","Chaofeng Sha","Xin Peng"]}},"tmdate":1747306386933,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731477758025,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xin_Peng6"],"forum":"c0Hhw7zjbi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":204589,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747306386933,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"c0Hhw7zjbi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICSE 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11029684/11029718/11029728.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICSE/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"spiess|calibration_and_correctness_of_language_models_for_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Claudio_Spiess1","~David_Gros1","~Kunal_Suresh_Pai1","~Michael_Pradel1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Md._Rafiqul_Islam_Rabin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Amin_Alipour:","~Susmit_Jha1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Prem_Devanbu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Toufique_Ahmed:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE55347.2025.00040"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icse/Spiess0PPRAJDA25,\n  author={Claudio Spiess and David Gros and Kunal Suresh Pai and Michael Pradel and Md. Rafiqul Islam Rabin and Amin Alipour and Susmit Jha and Prem Devanbu and Toufique Ahmed},\n  title={Calibration and Correctness of Language Models for Code},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={540-552},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE55347.2025.00040},\n  booktitle={ICSE},\n  crossref={conf/icse/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine learning models are widely used, but can also often be wrong. Users would benefit from a reliable indication of whether a given output from a given model should be trusted, so a rational decision can be made whether to use the output or not. For example, outputs can be associated with a confidence measure; if this confidence measure is strongly associated with likelihood of correctness, then the model is said to be well-calibrated. A well-calibrated confidence measure can serve as a basis for rational, graduated decision-making on how much review and care is needed when using generated code. Calibration has so far been studied in mostly non-generative (e.g., classification) settings, especially in software engineering. However, generated code can quite often be wrong: Given generated code, developers must decide whether to use directly, use after varying intensity of careful review, or discard model-generated code. Thus, calibration is vital in generative settings. We make several contributions. We develop a framework for evaluating the calibration of code-generating models. We consider several tasks, correctness criteria, datasets, and approaches, and find that, by and large, generative code models we test are not well-calibrated out of the box. We then show how calibration can be improved using standard methods, such as Platt scaling. Since Platt scaling relies on the prior availability of correctness data, we evaluate the applicability and generalizability of Platt scaling in software engineering, discuss settings where it has good potential for practical use, and settings where it does not. Our contributions will lead to better-calibrated decision-making in the current use of code generated by language models, and offers a framework for future research to further improve calibration methods for generative models in software engineering."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Correctness of Language Models for Code"},"authors":{"value":["Claudio Spiess","David Gros","Kunal Suresh Pai","Michael Pradel","Md. Rafiqul Islam Rabin","Amin Alipour","Susmit Jha","Prem Devanbu","Toufique Ahmed"]}},"tmdate":1788399890721,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1751726236067,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Susmit_Jha1"],"forum":"uS1g2crF3k","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":566089,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1788399890721,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uS1g2crF3k","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.22938v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|executionanchored_hallucination_calibration_reranking_for_verilog_code_generation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.22938"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2608-22938,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Guang Yang and Xing Hu and Xiang Chen and Terry Yue Zhuo and Xin Xia},\n  title={Execution-Anchored Hallucination Calibration Reranking for Verilog Code Generation},\n  year={2026},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1785542400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2608.22938},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.22938}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in code generation, yet their performance degrades significantly on low-resource Hardware Description Languages such as Verilog. While multi-candidate sampling improves the likelihood of generating correct solutions, au-tomatically selecting the optimal candidate remains an open challenge. Through a systematic empirical study across nine models and two benchmarks, we identify two critical limitations:(1) existing execution-based reranking methods, which rely on testbench pass/fail outcomes, exhibit poor domain transferability due to low-quality generated testbenches; and (2) LLM-as-a-Judge suffers from reasoning hallucination, producing incon-sistent judgments for execution-equivalent code. These findings reveal two signal types with orthogonal errors: execution signals(deterministic but testbench coverage limited)and reasoning signals (semantically rich but hallucination-prone). Their orthog-onality suggests combining the two signals, yet in our experiments letting the reasoner directly observe execution results merely anchors its judgments on test outcomes; we therefore acquire the two signals independently and fuse them only at the decision stage. Based on these insights, we propose EAHC, an Execution-Anchored Hallucination Calibration reranking framework that anchors reasoning judgments to execution behavior so that execution-equivalent candidates receive consistent scores, which implements a dual-channel architecture: EAHC-R, a 4B reasoning discriminator; and EAHC-T, a testbench generator leveraging RAG for execution verification."},"title":{"value":"Execution-Anchored Hallucination Calibration Reranking for Verilog Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Guang Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xing Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiang Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Terry Yue Zhuo","username":""},{"fullname":"Xin Xia","username":"~Xin_Xia11"}]}},"tmdate":1791774398811,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2608-22938"],"tcdate":1791774384111,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xin_Xia11"],"forum":"PCDYEcoibB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":171732,"cdate":1785542400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791774398811,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"PCDYEcoibB","version":2},{"content":{"checklist_submissions":{"value":["I confirm that the submission is anonymous.","I confirm that the submission does not exceed one page.","I confirm that the PDF is included.","I confirm that the submission is within the scope of WiML @ NeurIPS 2026."]},"venue":{"value":"WiML @ NeurIPS 2026"},"outreach_channel":{"value":["WiML google group mail / Email accouncement","Colleague / Advisor / Friend recommendation (Word of mouth)"]},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"home_institution":{"value":"North America"},"keywords":{"value":["large language models","hallucination","calibration","code optimization","abstention","LLM evaluation","benchmark design","AI for software engineering","trustworthy ML"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nwilson2026efficiency,\ntitle={Efficiency Hallucination: Measuring Behavioral Calibration in {LLM}-Based Code Optimization},\nauthor={Sarah Wilson and Gail Kaiser and Patrick Musau},\nbooktitle={Women in Machine Learning Workshop @ NeurIPS 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6gsuvHn3n1}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficiency Hallucination: Measuring Behavioral Calibration in LLM-Based Code Optimization"},"confirm_age":{"value":"Yes"},"public_preprint":{"value":"N/A"},"limit_of_paper":{"value":"Yes"},"paperhash":{"value":"wilson|efficiency_hallucination_measuring_behavioral_calibration_in_llmbased_code_optimization"},"email_sharing_with_WiML_consent":{"value":"Yes"},"residence":{"value":["United States"]},"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"WiML_Code_of_Conduct":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into automated code optimization introduces a reliability risk we term the Efficiency Hallucination: the generation of plausible code edits accompanied by confident, unverifiable performance claims on already-optimal code. Unlike functional hallucinations, these are invisible to per-commit test suites: the code compiles and passes tests, yet no performance improvement occurs. In production pipelines, such edits cost review time and erode trust in automated tooling."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6efba505e7f4804b9bba745b07394f106e91bdb2.pdf"},"anonymized_and_aggregated_information_consent":{"value":"Yes"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML"},"Funding_acknowledgement":{"value":"Yes"},"screening_acknowledgement":{"value":"Yes"},"preferred_presentation_hub":{"value":"Sydney, Australia (Main)"},"confirm_original_research":{"value":"Yes"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Sarah_Wilson2","fullname":"Sarah Wilson","institutions":[{"name":"Google","domain":"google.com","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Gail_Kaiser1","fullname":"Gail Kaiser","institutions":[{"name":"Columbia University","domain":"cs.columbia.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Patrick_Musau1","fullname":"Patrick Musau","institutions":[]}]}},"tmdate":1790773238391,"pdate":1790773197596,"tcdate":1787547938056,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/Submission527/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/Submission527/Authors"],"forum":"6gsuvHn3n1","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":527,"cdate":1787547938056,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1790773238391,"odate":1790773197596,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML","id":"6gsuvHn3n1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|caliqec_insitu_qubit_calibration_for_surface_code_quantum_error_correction"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3695053.3731042"},"title":{"value":"CaliQEC: In-situ Qubit Calibration for Surface Code Quantum Error Correction"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xiang Fang"},{"fullname":"Keyi Yin"},{"fullname":"Yuchen Zhu"},{"fullname":"Jixuan Ruan"},{"fullname":"Dean Tullsen"},{"fullname":"Zhiding Liang"},{"fullname":"Andrew Sornborger"},{"fullname":"Ang Li","username":"~Ang_Li11"},{"fullname":"Travis Humble"},{"fullname":"Yufei Ding"},{"fullname":"Yunong Shi"}]}},"tmdate":1789092605069,"pdate":1750464000000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3695053.3731042"],"tcdate":1769364751103,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ang_Li11"],"forum":"BtZC90DAjt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":38904,"cdate":1750442146571,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092605069,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"BtZC90DAjt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISCA 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISCA/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|caliqec_insitu_qubit_calibration_for_surface_code_quantum_error_correction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Fang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keyi_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuchen_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jixuan_Ruan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dean_Tullsen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiding_Liang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Sornborger:","~Ang_Li11","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Travis_S._Humble:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yufei_Ding_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yunong_Shi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3695053.3731042"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isca/FangYZRTLS0H0S25,\n  author={Xiang Fang and Keyi Yin and Yuchen Zhu and Jixuan Ruan and Dean Tullsen and Zhiding Liang and Andrew Sornborger and Ang Li and Travis S. Humble and Yufei Ding and Yunong Shi},\n  title={CaliQEC: In-situ Qubit Calibration for Surface Code Quantum Error Correction},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1402-1416},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3695053.3731042},\n  booktitle={ISCA},\n  crossref={conf/isca/2025}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"CaliQEC: In-situ Qubit Calibration for Surface Code Quantum Error Correction"},"authors":{"value":["Xiang Fang","Keyi Yin","Yuchen Zhu","Jixuan Ruan","Dean Tullsen","Zhiding Liang","Andrew Sornborger","Ang Li","Travis S. Humble","Yufei Ding","Yunong Shi"]}},"tmdate":1769364871286,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/isca/FangYZRTLS0H0S25"],"tcdate":1769364784551,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ang_Li11"],"forum":"9ruzjuUmAU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":800490,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769364871286,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9ruzjuUmAU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.31159v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"siddiq|an_empirical_study_of_security_calibration_in_large_language_models_for_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.31159"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-31159,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mohammed Latif Siddiq and Md Nafiu Rahman and Joanna C. S. Santos},\n  title={An Empirical Study of Security Calibration in Large Language Models for Code},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.31159},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.31159}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly transforming software development, yet their use in security-critical contexts raises a key question: do models know when their generated code is insecure? This property, known as calibration, measures whether a model's confidence aligns with the true correctness of its outputs. We present the first large-scale empirical study of security calibration in LLM-generated code. We evaluate GPT-4o-mini, Gemini-2.0-Flash, and Qwen3-Coder-Next across multiple temperature settings on two complementary benchmarks: self-contained security tasks and multi-language repository-level contexts. Our results suggest that overconfidence is prevalent across the evaluated LLMs. Functional calibration is consistently worse than security calibration, suggesting that models estimate security outcomes more reliably than functional correctness, potentially because functional correctness depends on complex execution behavior. We also examine whether calibration-guided automated repair can help remediate vulnerabilities in LLM-generated code, finding only limited improvements while frequently introducing functional regressions. Moreover, we study different mitigation strategies for reducing False Trust, where models assign high confidence to vulnerable code. The results show that although architectural gating improves calibration on controlled benchmarks, calibration deteriorates in realistic repository-level settings, increasing the risk of high-confidence vulnerable outputs."},"title":{"value":"An Empirical Study of Security Calibration in Large Language Models for Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Mohammed Latif Siddiq","username":""},{"fullname":"Md Nafiu Rahman","username":"~Md_Nafiu_Rahman1"},{"fullname":"Joanna C. S. Santos","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791810678602,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-31159"],"tcdate":1791810676626,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Md_Nafiu_Rahman1"],"forum":"aJj2QlNpEd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":173188,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791810678602,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aJj2QlNpEd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISSTA 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"qiu|calico_automated_knowledge_calibration_and_diagnosis_for_elevating_ai_mastery_in_code_tasks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3650212.3680399"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/issta/QiuHZ024,\n  author={Yuxin Qiu and Jie Hu and Qian Zhang and Heng Yin},\n  title={Calico: Automated Knowledge Calibration and Diagnosis for Elevating AI Mastery in Code Tasks},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1785-1797},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3650212.3680399},\n  booktitle={ISSTA},\n  crossref={conf/issta/2024}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Calico: Automated Knowledge Calibration and Diagnosis for Elevating AI Mastery in Code Tasks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuxin Qiu","username":""},{"fullname":"Jie Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Qian Zhang","username":"~Qian_Zhang51"},{"fullname":"Heng Yin","username":"~Heng_Yin1"}]}},"tmdate":1794243922092,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/issta/QiuHZ024"],"tcdate":1792105254952,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Heng_Yin1"],"forum":"DbGsnDRXsS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":191063,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1794243922092,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"DbGsnDRXsS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"qiu|calico_automated_knowledge_calibration_and_diagnosis_for_elevating_ai_mastery_in_code_tasks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3650212.3680399"},"title":{"value":"Calico: Automated Knowledge Calibration and Diagnosis for Elevating AI Mastery in Code Tasks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuxin Qiu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yuxin%20Qiu"},{"fullname":"Jie Hu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jie%20Hu"},{"fullname":"Qian Zhang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Qian%20Zhang"},{"fullname":"Heng Yin","username":"~Heng_Yin1"}]}},"tmdate":1792105015562,"pdate":1726012800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3650212.3680399"],"tcdate":1792105015394,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Heng_Yin1"],"forum":"0Qnp8mjE9R","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":127025,"cdate":1726059242132,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792105015562,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"0Qnp8mjE9R","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SCIA 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-21227-7_50.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SCIA/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"martynov|projector_calibration_by_inverse_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivan_Martynov:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Joni-Kristian_Kamarainen:","~Lasse_Lensu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-21227-7_50"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/scia/MartynovKL11,\n  author={Ivan Martynov and Joni-Kristian Kamarainen and Lasse Lensu},\n  title={Projector Calibration by \"Inverse Camera Calibration\"},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={536-544},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-21227-7_50},\n  booktitle={SCIA},\n  crossref={conf/scia/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The accuracy of 3-D reconstructions depends substantially on the accuracy of active vision system calibration. In this work, the problem of video projector calibration is solved by inverting the standard camera calibration work flow. The calibration procedure requires a single camera, which does not need to be calibrated and which is used as the sensor whether projected dots and calibration pattern landmarks, such as the checkerboard corners, coincide. The method iteratively adjusts the projected dots to coincide with the landmarks and the final coordinates are used as inputs to a camera calibration method. The otherwise slow iterative adjustment is accelerated by estimating a plane homography between the detected landmarks and the projected dots, which makes the calibration method fast."},"title":{"value":"Projector Calibration by \"Inverse Camera Calibration\""},"authors":{"value":["Ivan Martynov","Joni-Kristian Kamarainen","Lasse Lensu"]}},"tmdate":1769319325902,"pdate":1325289600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/scia/MartynovKL11"],"tcdate":1769319303096,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lasse_Lensu1"],"forum":"GAo9w7ArNv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":794903,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769319325902,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GAo9w7ArNv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Trans. Mach. Learn. Res. 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TMLR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"bohdal|metacalibration_learning_of_model_calibration_using_differentiable_expected_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ondrej_Bohdal:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongxin_Yang:","~Timothy_M._Hospedales2"]},"html":{"value":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=R2hUure38l"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tmlr/BohdalYH23,\n  author={Ondrej Bohdal and Yongxin Yang and Timothy M. Hospedales},\n  title={Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={Trans. Mach. Learn. Res.},\n  volume={2023},\n  url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=R2hUure38l}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error"},"authors":{"value":["Ondrej Bohdal","Yongxin Yang","Timothy M. Hospedales"]}},"tmdate":1741122639665,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1741122628111,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Timothy_Hospedales1"],"forum":"OQnWCiBbwL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":350085,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741122639665,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OQnWCiBbwL","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/ondrejbohdal/meta-calibration"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6ccf07be9d623bc1bf0de0a865400ecdcb1ad28f.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration of neural networks is a topical problem that is becoming more and more important as neural networks increasingly underpin real-world applications. The problem is especially noticeable when using modern neural networks, for which there is a significant difference between the confidence of the model and the probability of correct prediction. Various strategies have been proposed to improve calibration, yet accurate calibration remains challenging. We propose a novel framework with two contributions: introducing a new differentiable surrogate for expected calibration error (DECE) that allows calibration quality to be directly optimised, and a meta-learning framework that uses DECE to optimise for validation set calibration with respect to model hyper-parameters. The results show that we achieve competitive performance with existing calibration approaches. Our framework opens up a new avenue and toolset for tackling calibration, which we believe will inspire further work on this important challenge."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nbohdal2023metacalibration,\ntitle={Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error},\nauthor={Ondrej Bohdal and Yongxin Yang and Timothy Hospedales},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=R2hUure38l},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Meta-Calibration: Learning of Model Calibration Using Differentiable Expected Calibration Error"},"license":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f9266913213b59a701bf7cab86d908318fa544ab.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"bohdal|metacalibration_learning_of_model_calibration_using_differentiable_expected_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ondrej_Bohdal1","~Yongxin_Yang1","~Timothy_Hospedales1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Yingzhen_Li1"},"authors":{"value":["Ondrej Bohdal","Yongxin Yang","Timothy Hospedales"]}},"tmdate":1726599551414,"pdate":1692835634087,"tcdate":1684868353601,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper1189/Authors"],"forum":"R2hUure38l","number":1189,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1684868353601,"mdate":1726599551414,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper1189/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/Paper1189/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"odate":1685036560103,"domain":"TMLR","id":"R2hUure38l","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA 2026)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8fe90612244dcafdbd3519c436adf488497d69e3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"feng|persuasive_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yiding_Feng1","~Wei_Tang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.03211"},"abstract":{"value":"We introduce and study the persuasive calibration problem, where a principal aims to provide trustworthy predictions about underlying events to a downstream agent to make desired\ndecisions. We adopt the standard calibration framework that regulates predictions to be unbiased conditional on their own value, and thus, they can reliably be interpreted at the face\nvalue by the agent. Allowing a small calibration error budget, we aim to answer the following\nquestion: what is and how to compute the optimal predictor under this calibration error budget,\nespecially when there exists incentive misalignment between the principal and the agent? We\nfocus on standard ℓt-norm Expected Calibration Error (ECE) metric.\n\nWe develop a general framework by viewing predictors as post-processed versions of perfectly\ncalibrated predictors. Using this framework, we first characterize the structure of the optimal\npredictor. Specifically, when the principal’s utility is event-independent and for ℓ1-norm ECE, we\nshow: (1) the optimal predictor is over-(resp. under-) confident for high (resp. low) true expected\noutcomes, while remaining perfectly calibrated in the middle; (2) the miscalibrated predictions\nexhibit a collinearity structure with the principal’s utility function. On the algorithmic side, we\nprovide a FPTAS for computing approximately optimal predictor for general principal utility\nand general ℓt-norm ECE. Moreover, for the ℓ1- and ℓ∞-norm ECE, we provide polynomial-time\nalgorithms that compute the exact optimal predictor."},"title":{"value":"Persuasive Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yiding Feng","Wei Tang"]}},"tmdate":1778039027216,"pdate":1764518400000,"tcdate":1778039027216,"writers":["~Yiding_Feng1","~Wei_Tang1"],"signatures":["~Wei_Tang1"],"forum":"0AY17SITMn","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":48745,"cdate":1778039027216,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778039027216,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"0AY17SITMn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neurocomputing 2013"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJON/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"leng|palmhash_code_vs_palmphasor_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lu_Leng:","~Jiashu_Zhang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2012.08.028"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijon/LengZ13,\n  author={Lu Leng and Jiashu Zhang},\n  title={PalmHash Code vs. PalmPhasor Code},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  journal={Neurocomputing},\n  volume={108},\n  pages={1-12},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2012.08.028}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The existing palmprint protection schemes without verification degrading do not strictly meet non-invertible requirement; while non-invertible palmprint protection schemes do not meet verification performance requirement in stolen-token scenario. PalmHash Code and PalmPhasor Code, as two cancelable palmprint coding schemes, are proposed in this paper to balance the conflict between security and verification performance. In order to reduce computational complexity and storage cost, cancelable palmprint coding frameworks are extended from one dimension to two dimensions. Besides, two measures, perpendicular orientation transposition and multi-orientation score level fusion, are employed to further improve the performance of two dimensional (2D) cancelable palmprint codes. PalmHash Code and PalmPhasor Code are compared extensively in this paper. The experimental results and analysis confirm that 2D PalmHash Code and 2D PalmPhasor Code enhanced by the two measures are effective and secure even in stolen-token scenario."},"title":{"value":"PalmHash Code vs. PalmPhasor Code"},"authors":{"value":["Lu Leng","Jiashu Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1741673532284,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1741673391444,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiashu_Zhang1"],"forum":"p2CIX8RaGj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":368196,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741673532284,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"p2CIX8RaGj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.10737v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|commenting_higherlevel_code_unit_full_code_reduced_code_or_hierarchical_code_summarization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weisong_Sun:","~Yiran_Zhang11","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhihui_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chunrong_Fang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yonglong_Zhang:","~Yebo_Feng1","~Jiangping_Huang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingya_Wang:","~Zhi_Jin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liu_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.10737"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-10737,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Weisong Sun and Yiran Zhang and Jie Zhu and Zhihui Wang and Chunrong Fang and Yonglong Zhang and Yebo Feng and Jiangping Huang and Xingya Wang and Zhi Jin and Yang Liu},\n  title={Commenting Higher-level Code Unit: Full Code, Reduced Code, or Hierarchical Code Summarization},\n  year={2025},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1740787200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2503.10737},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.10737}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Commenting code is a crucial activity in software development, as it aids in facilitating future maintenance and updates. To enhance the efficiency of writing comments and reduce developers' workload, researchers has proposed various automated code summarization (ACS) techniques to automatically generate comments/summaries for given code units. However, these ACS techniques primarily focus on generating summaries for code units at the method level. There is a significant lack of research on summarizing higher-level code units, such as file-level and module-level code units, despite the fact that summaries of these higher-level code units are highly useful for quickly gaining a macro-level understanding of software components and architecture. To fill this gap, in this paper, we conduct a systematic study on how to use LLMs for commenting higher-level code units, including file level and module level. These higher-level units are significantly larger than method-level ones, which poses challenges in handling long code inputs within LLM constraints and maintaining efficiency. To address these issues, we explore various summarization strategies for ACS of higher-level code units, which can be divided into three types: full code summarization, reduced code summarization, and hierarchical code summarization. The experimental results suggest that for summarizing file-level code units, using the full code is the most effective approach, with reduced code serving as a cost-efficient alternative. However, for summarizing module-level code units, hierarchical code summarization becomes the most promising strategy. In addition, inspired by the research on method-level ACS, we also investigate using the LLM as an evaluator to evaluate the quality of summaries of higher-level code units. The experimental results demonstrate that the LLM's evaluation results strongly correlate with human evaluations."},"title":{"value":"Commenting Higher-level Code Unit: Full Code, Reduced Code, or Hierarchical Code Summarization"},"authors":{"value":["Weisong Sun","Yiran Zhang","Jie Zhu","Zhihui Wang","Chunrong Fang","Yonglong Zhang","Yebo Feng","Jiangping Huang","Xingya Wang","Zhi Jin","Yang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1791419749560,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747645539154,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhi_Jin1"],"forum":"g1RUXRb43h","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":525749,"cdate":1740787200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791419749560,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"g1RUXRb43h","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.20159v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|axiom_benchmarking_llmasajudge_for_code_via_rulebased_perturbation_and_multisource_quality_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Chun_Yong_Chong1","~Xin_Xia11",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.20159"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2512-20159,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ruiqi Wang and Xinchen Wang and Cuiyun Gao and Chun Yong Chong and Xin Xia and Qing Liao},\n  title={AXIOM: Benchmarking LLM-as-a-Judge for Code via Rule-Based Perturbation and Multisource Quality Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={December},\n  cdate={1764547200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2512.20159},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.20159}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have been increasingly deployed in real-world software engineering, fostering the development of code evaluation metrics to study the quality of LLM-generated code. Conventional rule-based metrics merely score programs based on their surface-level similarities with reference programs instead of analyzing functionality and code quality in depth. To address this limitation, researchers have developed LLM-as-a-judge metrics, prompting LLMs to evaluate and score code, and curated various code evaluation benchmarks to validate their effectiveness. However, these benchmarks suffer from critical limitations, hindering reliable assessments of evaluation capability: Some feature coarse-grained binary labels, which reduce rich code behavior to a single bit of information, obscuring subtle errors. Others propose fine-grained but subjective, vaguely-defined evaluation criteria, introducing unreliability in manually-annotated scores, which is the ground-truth they rely on. Furthermore, they often use uncontrolled data synthesis methods, leading to unbalanced score distributions that poorly represent real-world code generation scenarios. To curate a diverse benchmark with programs of well-balanced distributions across various quality levels and streamline the manual annotation procedure, we propose AXIOM, a novel perturbation-based framework for synthesizing code evaluation benchmarks at scale. It reframes program scores as the refinement effort needed for deployment, consisting of two stages: (1) Rule-guided perturbation, which prompts LLMs to apply sequences of predefined perturbation rules to existing high-quality programs to modify their functionality and code quality, enabling us to precisely control each program's target score to achieve balanced score distributions. (2) Multisource quality calibration, which first selects a subset of..."},"title":{"value":"AXIOM: Benchmarking LLM-as-a-Judge for Code via Rule-Based Perturbation and Multisource Quality Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Ruiqi Wang","Xinchen Wang","Cuiyun Gao","Chun Yong Chong","Xin Xia","Qing Liao"]}},"tmdate":1791774385881,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2512-20159"],"tcdate":1772153374100,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chun_Yong_Chong1"],"forum":"ETI89hN8sR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":830465,"cdate":1764547200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791774385881,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ETI89hN8sR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2023 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Quantification","Calibration","Decision Making","Probabilistic Forecasting"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/901aa2707746a1ce0fc6f624a052b26cc85429ed.pdf"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nmarx2023calibration,\ntitle={Calibration by Distribution Matching: Trainable Kernel Calibration Metrics},\nauthor={Charles Thomas Marx and Sofian Zalouk and Stefano Ermon},\nbooktitle={Thirty-seventh Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=IhxD94i5ra}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration by Distribution Matching: Trainable Kernel Calibration Metrics"},"paperhash":{"value":"marx|calibration_by_distribution_matching_trainable_kernel_calibration_metrics"},"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a unifying framework for calibration metrics as distribution matching objectives."},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration ensures that probabilistic forecasts meaningfully capture uncertainty by requiring that predicted probabilities align with empirical frequencies. However, many existing calibration methods are specialized for post-hoc recalibration, which can worsen the sharpness of forecasts. Drawing on the insight that calibration can be viewed as a distribution matching task, we introduce kernel-based calibration metrics that unify and generalize popular forms of calibration for both classification and regression. These metrics admit differentiable sample estimates, making it easy to incorporate a calibration objective into empirical risk minimization. Furthermore, we provide intuitive mechanisms to tailor calibration metrics to a decision task, and enforce accurate loss estimation and no regret decisions. Our empirical evaluation demonstrates that employing these metrics as regularizers enhances calibration, sharpness, and decision-making across a range of regression and classification tasks, outperforming methods relying solely on post-hoc recalibration."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/60cbf4aa3028c293436a1b2eb89ec763db85bb48.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Charles_Thomas_Marx1","~Sofian_Zalouk1","~Stefano_Ermon1"]},"authors":{"value":["Charles Thomas Marx","Sofian Zalouk","Stefano Ermon"]}},"tmdate":1698949732916,"pdate":1695325912149,"tcdate":1683696418317,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission5967/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission5967/Authors"],"forum":"IhxD94i5ra","number":5967,"cdate":1683696418317,"mdate":1698949732916,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission5967/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission5967/-/Supplementary_Material_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission5967/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1698949732904,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","id":"IhxD94i5ra","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICC 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4024074/4024253/04024302.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICC/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"gounai|performance_of_concatenated_code_with_ldpc_code_and_rsc_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Satoshi_Gounai:","~Tomoaki_Ohtsuki2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Toshinobu_Kaneko:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICC.2006.254910"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icc/GounaiOK06,\n  author={Satoshi Gounai and Tomoaki Ohtsuki and Toshinobu Kaneko},\n  title={Performance of Concatenated Code with LDPC Code and RSC Code},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={1195-1199},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICC.2006.254910},\n  booktitle={ICC},\n  crossref={conf/icc/2006}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we propose a concatenated code by combining a Recursive Systematic Convolutional (RSC) code with a Low-Density Parity-Check (LDPC) code. The proposed concatenated code is encoded in parallel by an RSC encoder and an LDPC encoder without interleavers between them. When decoding the proposed concatenated code, the information bits are decoded by two decoding algorithms, and soft information is exchanged between the RSC decoder and the LDPC decoder. This allows for the elimination of wrong codewords output by each decoder. The use of an LDPC code allows us to halt the decoding process when the valid codeword is obtained. We evaluate the error rate performance of the proposed concatenated code by computer simulations. We show that the proposed concatenated code does not have a high error floor, and achieves better BLock Error Rate (BLER) performance than either conventional LDPC codes or conventional turbo codes in the high Eb/No region. We also show that since the proposed concatenated code can correct wrong codewords output by each decoder, it achieves better BLER than the regular LDPC code at the same Bit Error Rate (BER)."},"title":{"value":"Performance of Concatenated Code with LDPC Code and RSC Code"},"authors":{"value":["Satoshi Gounai","Tomoaki Ohtsuki","Toshinobu Kaneko"]}},"tmdate":1762753959981,"pdate":1136073600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icc/GounaiOK06"],"tcdate":1762753823193,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~TOMOAKI_OHTSUKI1"],"forum":"0zNgyQA6XK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":685227,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762753959981,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0zNgyQA6XK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICC 2006"},"venueid":{"value":"DBLP.org"},"paperhash":{"value":"gounai|performance_of_concatenated_code_with_ldpc_code_and_rsc_code"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Tomoaki_Ohtsuki2",""]},"title":{"value":"Performance of Concatenated Code with LDPC Code and RSC Code."},"authors":{"value":["Satoshi Gounai","Tomoaki Ohtsuki","Toshinobu Kaneko"]}},"tmdate":1762753828538,"tcdate":1762753828538,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~TOMOAKI_OHTSUKI1"],"forum":"iYbeiMEqqj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":685324,"cdate":1762753828538,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record"],"mdate":1762753828538,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iYbeiMEqqj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICONIP (4) 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-36808-1_42.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICONIP/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|code_generation_from_supervised_code_embeddings"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiuyuan_Chen:","~Zhaoyi_Liu4"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-36808-1_42"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iconip/HuCL19,\n  author={Han Hu and Qiuyuan Chen and Zhaoyi Liu},\n  title={Code Generation from Supervised Code Embeddings},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={388-396},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-36808-1_42},\n  booktitle={ICONIP (4)},\n  crossref={conf/iconip/2019-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation, which generates source code from natural language, is beneficial for constructing smarter Integrated Development Environments (IDEs), retrieving code more effectively and so on. Traditional approaches are based on matching similar code snippets, and recently researchers pay more attention to machine learning, especially the encoder-decoder framework. Faced with code generation, most encoder-decoder frameworks suffer from two drawbacks: (a) The length of the code snippet is always much longer than the length of its corresponding natural language, which makes it hard to align them, especially for encoders at word level; (b) Code snippets with the same functionality could be implemented in various ways, even completely different at word level. For drawback (a), we propose a new Supervised Code Embedding (SCE) model to promote the alignment between natural language and code. For drawback (b), with the help of Abstract Syntax Tree (AST), we propose a new distributed representation of code snippets which overcomes this drawback. To evaluate our approaches, we build a variant of the encoder-decoder model to generates code with the help of pre-trained code embedding. We perform experiments on several open source datasets. The experiment results indicate that our approaches are effective and outperform the state-of-the-art."},"title":{"value":"Code Generation from Supervised Code Embeddings"},"authors":{"value":["Han Hu","Qiuyuan Chen","Zhaoyi Liu"]}},"tmdate":1762407986804,"pdate":1546300800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iconip/HuCL19"],"tcdate":1762407977267,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~ZHAOYI_Liu2"],"forum":"9zC7zX1nFQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":662587,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762407986804,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9zC7zX1nFQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.15778v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|agentic_confidence_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15778"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2601-15778,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jiaxin Zhang and Caiming Xiong and Chien-Sheng Wu},\n  title={Agentic Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2601.15778},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.15778}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"AI agents are rapidly advancing from passive language models to autonomous systems executing complex, multi-step tasks. Yet their overconfidence in failure remains a fundamental barrier to deployment in high-stakes settings. Existing calibration methods, built for static single-turn outputs, cannot address the unique challenges of agentic systems, such as compounding errors along trajectories, uncertainty from external tools, and opaque failure modes. To address these challenges, we introduce, for the first time, the problem of Agentic Confidence Calibration and propose Holistic Trajectory Calibration (HTC), a novel diagnostic framework that extracts rich process-level features ranging from macro dynamics to micro stability across an agent's entire trajectory. Powered by a simple, interpretable model, HTC consistently surpasses strong baselines in both calibration and discrimination, across eight benchmarks, multiple LLMs, and diverse agent frameworks. Beyond performance, HTC delivers three essential advances: it provides interpretability by revealing the signals behind failure, enables transferability by applying across domains without retraining, and achieves generalization through a General Agent Calibrator (GAC) that achieves the best calibration (lowest ECE) on the out-of-domain GAIA benchmark. Together, these contributions establish a new process-centric paradigm for confidence calibration, providing a framework for diagnosing and enhancing the reliability of AI agents."},"title":{"value":"Agentic Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jiaxin Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Caiming Xiong","username":""},{"fullname":"Chien-Sheng Wu","username":"~Chien-Sheng_Wu1"}]}},"tmdate":1792098481010,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2601-15778"],"tcdate":1792098472057,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Chien-Sheng_Wu1"],"forum":"Ap98r9xNMX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":190605,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792098481010,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Ap98r9xNMX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Agents","Confidence","Reliability","Calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/ef551ddaaec94bf963dfdd08a03782a0d2a02a26.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhang2026agentic,\ntitle={Agentic Confidence Calibration},\nauthor={Jiaxin Zhang and Caiming Xiong and Chien-Sheng Wu},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=B1ISNZQHuI}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Agentic Confidence Calibration"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|agentic_confidence_calibration"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/3d2ba9849d1646224b0ed4dba2adeac878abbcae.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"social_aspects->accountability_transparency_and_interpretability"},"abstract":{"value":"AI agents are rapidly advancing from passive language models to autonomous systems executing complex, multi-step tasks. Yet their overconfidence in failure remains a fundamental barrier to deployment in high-stakes settings. Existing calibration methods, built for static single-turn outputs, cannot address the unique challenges of agentic systems, such as compounding errors along trajectories, uncertainty from external tools, and opaque failure modes. To address these challenges, we introduce, for the first time, the problem of Agentic Confidence Calibration and propose Holistic Trajectory Calibration (HTC), a novel diagnostic framework that extracts rich process-level features ranging from macro dynamics to micro stability across an agent's entire trajectory. Powered by a simple, interpretable model, HTC consistently surpasses strong baselines in both calibration and discrimination, across eight benchmarks, multiple LLMs, and diverse agent frameworks. Beyond performance, HTC delivers three essential advances: it provides interpretability by revealing the signals behind failure, enables transferability by applying across domains without retraining, and achieves generalization through a General Agent Calibrator (GAC) that achieves the best calibration (lowest ECE) on the out-of-domain GAIA benchmark. Together, these contributions establish a new process-centric paradigm for confidence calibration, providing a framework for diagnosing and enhancing the reliability of AI agents."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b2c1149bcb05cc7914d93995e3febf22e8d1e231.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"AI systems are increasingly able to act like assistants: they can plan, use tools, and work through complex tasks step by step. But these systems can still make mistakes while sounding very confident, which is especially risky in settings where people rely on them for important decisions. We study how to tell when an AI assistant’s final answer should be trusted. Our key idea is to look beyond the final answer and examine the whole process the system followed to reach it. Similar to checking a student’s work rather than only grading the final result, our method looks for warning signs across the steps of an AI assistant’s reasoning, such as unstable decisions or sudden changes in confidence. Using these signals, we build a lightweight tool that gives a more reliable estimate of whether the assistant is likely to be correct. This can help people better understand when an AI system may need extra checking. Our results suggest a practical way to make AI assistants safer, more transparent, and better able to recognize when they might be wrong."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiaxin_Zhang2","~Caiming_Xiong1","~Chien-Sheng_Wu1"]},"authors":{"value":["Jiaxin Zhang","Caiming Xiong","Chien-Sheng Wu"]}},"tmdate":1790067372635,"pdate":1777576532576,"tcdate":1769106500450,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17301/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17301/Authors"],"forum":"B1ISNZQHuI","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":17301,"cdate":1769106500450,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17301/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17301/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067372635,"odate":1782341966745,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"B1ISNZQHuI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Quantification","Conformal Prediction","Calibration"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ndoula2026conformal,\ntitle={Conformal Calibration Transfer},\nauthor={Achref Doula},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=nw5aekrzBa}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Conformal Calibration Transfer"},"paperhash":{"value":"doula|conformal_calibration_transfer"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/ce8f498724086651e00277fc96e757f7c934a588.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic_methods"},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction converts point predictions into set-valued predictions with coverage guarantees under exchangeability between calibration and deployment data. We study *conformal calibration transfer*, where this requirement fails because labeled calibration is available only in a source space, while prediction sets are needed in a target space linked to the source through *unlabeled paired* observations (e.g., paired modalities or sensor changes).\nWe propose Transported Conformal Calibration (TCC): we transport labeled source calibration into the target space using the paired data, and then correct residual post-transport mismatch using only unlabeled target inputs. We instantiate this correction with two complementary methods: **TCC-KS**, which uses a label-free uncertainty surrogate to detect mismatch and adjust calibration conservatively, and **weighted-TCC**, which reweights transported calibration toward the target domain for improved efficiency when weights are stable. We provide finite-sample target-domain coverage guarantees that adapt to an observable measure of mismatch. Across CIFAR-100-C, Tiny-ImageNet-C, and SEN12MS, we show reliable target-domain coverage transfer without labeled target calibration data, with label-free diagnostics that predict when correction is needed."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://gitlab.com/achref.d/tcc"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8b0d56ba9ad3c8cc7e4bb9a5bf183ad929ce523c.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Conformal prediction can turn a model’s output into a set of plausible answers with a formal guarantee that the correct answer is included often enough. To achieve this, it typically requires a labeled calibration set from the same kind of data on which the model will be used. In many practical settings, however, labeled calibration examples may exist only in one input space, while prediction sets are needed in another. For example, the two spaces may correspond to different data representations, modalities, sensors, or processing pipelines, and collecting new labels in the target space may be expensive, delayed, or unavailable.\n\nThis paper studies how to transfer conformal calibration from a labeled source space to a target space using unlabeled paired observations. The proposed framework, Transported Conformal Calibration (TCC), first maps source calibration examples into the target input space and then corrects remaining mismatch using diagnostics that do not require target labels. It combines a conservative safeguard for reliability with a reweighting strategy that can improve efficiency when the transferred calibration data closely match the target domain.\n\nBy reducing the need for labeled target calibration data, this work broadens the situations in which conformal prediction can be used. It also provides practical signals indicating when calibration transfer is reliable and when more conservative prediction sets are appropriate."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Achref_Doula1"]},"authors":{"value":["Achref Doula"]}},"tmdate":1790067333445,"pdate":1777576345655,"tcdate":1768922177572,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10054/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10054/Authors"],"forum":"nw5aekrzBa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":10054,"cdate":1768922177572,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10054/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067333445,"odate":1782341936956,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"nw5aekrzBa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCNN 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"feng|deep_kernel_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN60899.2024.10649932"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcnn/FengXQLWX24,\n  author={Zunlei Feng and Wenxiang Xu and Tian Qiu and Jie Lei and Huiqiong Wang and Zhongle Xie},\n  title={Deep Kernel Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN60899.2024.10649932},\n  booktitle={IJCNN},\n  crossref={conf/ijcnn/2024}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Deep Kernel Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zunlei Feng","username":""},{"fullname":"Wenxiang Xu","username":""},{"fullname":"Tian Qiu","username":""},{"fullname":"Jie Lei","username":""},{"fullname":"Huiqiong Wang","username":"~Huiqiong_Wang1"},{"fullname":"Zhongle Xie","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1788005881392,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ijcnn/FengXQLWX24"],"tcdate":1788005880963,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Huiqiong_Wang1"],"forum":"YI5WhMaDEk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":144738,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1788005881392,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"YI5WhMaDEk","version":2}],"count":10000}